🚀 Week 6 – Day 2 Wildcards & Bounded Types in Java Generics ☕

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🧠 Introduction

Wildcards and Bounded Types make Java Generics more flexible and type-safe.

👉 They help:

✔ Work with unknown types
✔ Restrict allowed data types
✔ Increase code reusability
✔ Improve type safety

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 1. What are Wildcards?

A wildcard is represented using:

```java id="w1"
?
```

👉 It represents an unknown type.

---

📌 Example

```java id="w2"
List<?> list;
```

This list can hold any type of object.

---

🎯 Why Use Wildcards?

Without Wildcards:

```java id="w3"
List<String> names =
        new ArrayList<>();
```

Only `String` objects are allowed.

---

With Wildcards:

```java id="w4"
List<?> data;
```

Can reference:

* List<String>
* List<Integer>
* List<Double>

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 2. Unbounded Wildcards

Allows any type.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 Syntax

```java id="w5"
List<?> list;
```

---

💻 Example

```java id="w6"
import java.util.*;

public class Demo {

    static void display(List<?> list) {

        for (Object obj : list) {
            System.out.println(obj);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        List<String> names =
                Arrays.asList("Java", "Python");

        display(names);
    }
}
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 3. Upper Bounded Wildcards

Restricts types to a class or its subclasses.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 Syntax

```java id="w7"
<? extends ClassName>
```

---

💻 Example

```java id="w8"
List<? extends Number> numbers;
```

---

✔ Allowed Types

```java id="w9"
List<Integer> list1;
List<Double> list2;
List<Float> list3;
```

---

❌ Not Allowed

```java id="w10"
List<String> list;
```

---

📌 Example Program

```java id="w11"
static double sum(
        List<? extends Number> list) {

    double total = 0;

    for (Number n : list) {
        total += n.doubleValue();
    }

    return total;
}
```

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 4. Lower Bounded Wildcards

Restricts types to a class or its superclasses.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 Syntax

```java id="w12"
<? super ClassName>
```

---

💻 Example

```java id="w13"
List<? super Integer> list;
```

---

✔ Allowed Types

```java id="w14"
List<Integer> list1;
List<Number> list2;
List<Object> list3;
```

---

❌ Not Allowed

```java id="w15"
List<Double> list;
```

---

📌 Example Program

```java id="w16"
static void addNumbers(
        List<? super Integer> list) {

    list.add(10);
    list.add(20);
}
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 5. Bounded Type Parameters

Used to restrict generic types.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 Syntax

```java id="b1"
class ClassName<T extends Type> {
}
```

---

💻 Example

```java id="b2"
class Calculator<T extends Number> {

    T number;

    Calculator(T number) {
        this.number = number;
    }

    void display() {
        System.out.println(number);
    }
}
```

---

✔ Valid Usage

```java id="b3"
Calculator<Integer> c1 =
        new Calculator<>(100);

Calculator<Double> c2 =
        new Calculator<>(99.9);
```

---

❌ Invalid Usage

```java id="b4"
Calculator<String> c3 =
        new Calculator<>("Java");
```

---

🔹 Multiple Bounds

A type can have multiple bounds.

---

📌 Example

```java id="b5"
class Demo<T extends Number & Comparable<T>> {
}
```

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📊 Wildcards vs Bounded Types

| Feature     | Wildcards     | Bounded Types           |
| ----------- | ------------- | ----------------------- |
| Symbol      | ?             | T                       |
| Purpose     | Unknown types | Restrict generic types  |
| Flexibility | High          | Controlled              |
| Usage       | Collections   | Generic Classes/Methods |

